  "intro": "The Bengali script: vowels and vowel signs, the consonants, special signs and conjuncts, numerals, the main reading rules, and a key to the symbols in the pronunciations.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How the Bengali script works",
      "text": [
        "Bengali is written from left to right in its own script, a cousin of the Devanagari used for Hindi. Letters hang from a line along the top.",
        "Each consonant carries a built-in vowel, অ: ক on its own is kɔ or ko. Other vowels after a consonant are shown with a vowel sign attached to it; a vowel at the start of a word uses its full letter. Some signs are written before the consonant even though they are said after it.",
        "Two consonants with no vowel between them are usually joined into one conjunct letter: ক + ষ = ক্ষ, ন + দ = ন্দ."
      ]
    },
